## Changes since the first upload (7.2.6-piano5)

- Kernel `7.2.6-piano7`:
  - Wi-Fi: ath12k applies the vendor SAR TX power limit to the WCN7860, as stock Android does, so
    5 GHz 80 MHz links no longer drop about 12 s after association. Verified on the test unit with the
    two changed modules on 7.2.6-piano6 (80 MHz HE for 120 s, 50 MB at 74.7 Mbit/s down and 100 Mbit/s
    up, 2.4 GHz unchanged), not yet with this kernel package.
  - Volume keys (PM8550 GPIO6 and the PMK8550 resin, as in the stock devicetree) and the PM8550
    temperature-alarm thermal zone; not yet tested on the device.
  - Configuration that Ubuntu's generic kernel has: PSI (for systemd-oomd), zram, uhid and hidraw
    (Bluetooth LE input devices), nftables and the common xtables modules, WireGuard, the Yama and
    Landlock security modules, NTFS3, CIFS, ISO 9660, UDF, dm-crypt and binfmt_misc. Not tested on the
    device; no BTF type information (built without pahole).
  - Since 7.2.6-piano6: the four speakers (see below).
- Speakers: stereo streams are up-mixed to the bottom pair at -3 dB (`channelmix.upmix-method = simple`);
  with PipeWire's default method `none` the bottom pair stayed silent. Checked in QEMU, not yet heard
  on the tablet.
- Clock: a boot starts from the time `fake-hwclock` saved at the last shutdown or hourly instead of
  systemd's built-in date (2026-04-15), and chrony skips the NTS certificate time checks until its
  first update (`nocerttimecheck 1`). chrony selects sources regardless of authentication
  (`authselectmode ignore`), so that it also synchronises behind a router that answers every NTP request
  itself (not yet tested); unauthenticated time is then accepted when NTS is unavailable. These are
  interim measures until the clock offset can be stored on the PMIC.
- Developer mode stays off in the image; the installation guide turns it on for the installation and
  off at the end of step 10. Its SSH socket accepts connections on the USB interface `usb0` only, not
  over Wi-Fi.
- Only the piano kernel is installed: Ubuntu's generic HWE kernel, its headers, tools and zfs modules
  and `kdump-tools` are removed (47 packages, including `libc6-dev`: install `build-essential` before
  compiling), so updates no longer download kernels that cannot boot and build initrds for them.
  `linux-firmware` stays.
- Swap in compressed RAM (zram): half the memory up to 8 GiB, zstd.
- The login screen and Initial Setup use 200 % scaling (one system-wide `monitors.xml`); not yet
  checked on the device.
- Kernel and initrd updates without `/etc/piano/boot-partitions.json`, such as the one Initial Setup's
  keyboard page starts, leave the boot set and slot A unchanged instead of failing and leaving `dracut`
  half-configured; `piano-boot-switch` reads the serial from `/proc/bootconfig` and accepts the
  release set.
- Installation: Fastboot writes only the used blocks of the root image, and the build discards the
  blocks it freed, about 8.7 GB in a check build of this root. The first upload stored the free space
  as 51.8 GiB of FILL, which the tablet kept writing for about 30 minutes after `OKAY`.
- Hostname `xiaomipad-8pro` with the pretty name "Xiaomi Pad 8 Pro" (Initial Setup sets the hostname
  from its *Computer* field); no stale APT indexes: run `sudo apt update` before the first `apt install`.

## What works

Status on the test unit (one 512 GB unit, model 25091RP04C) with 7.2.6-piano5 and 7.2.6-piano6;
`$kernel_release` has not been booted there yet. See the [support matrix]($repo_url/blob/$integration_commit/README.md#support-matrix) for the details and limits.

- ✅ Working: CPU frequency scaling, boot from slot A (marked successful after each boot), UFS storage,
  GNOME on Wayland at 200 % scaling, Adreno 830 rendering with the bundled Mesa 26.2.0, battery gauges
  and thermal zones, SSH over USB in developer mode.
- 🟡 Partial: display (firmware framebuffer, 3200×2136 at 120 Hz, brightness only through sysfs, no screen-off),
  touchscreen (single finger, needs your calibration), magnetic keyboard (keys), Wi-Fi (association, DHCP
  and data on 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz verified with 7.2.6-piano6, 80 MHz with this kernel's fix loaded as
  modules; not yet with this kernel package), Bluetooth (discovery, needs your
  address), internal microphones (capture verified acoustically: a 1 kHz test tone was recorded by all four
  microphones; speech quality and noise processing unverified), speakers (four amplifiers through the
  TDM path at a fixed gain under a kernel cap; a 1 kHz test tone played by them was recorded by the
  internal microphones; no speaker protection, listening tests and channel mapping pending), switching
  between Ubuntu and Android, battery and charger reporting (read-only; no charge control), power key
  (volume keys described from the stock devicetree, untested).
- ❌ Unsupported: charging (the battery discharges under Linux, also on USB), cameras, sensors,
  video decoding, USB host and external display, suspend, writing the real-time clock (the clock resumes
  from the last shutdown and is behind after every boot), fingerprint, stylus.

## Per-unit data you must add

The image and the boot set contain no data of any unit: no account, serial number, Bluetooth or Wi-Fi
address, touch calibration, boot profile, partition map or signing key of yours. Until you add them:

| File in `/etc/piano/` (root, mode 0600) | Without it |
|---|---|
| `thp-calibration.json` | the touchscreen does not start (use the keyboard) |
| `wlan-address` | Wi-Fi uses a random MAC address on every boot: this unit's WCN7860 reports an all-zero address, and ath12k falls back to a random one unless the boot DTB carries the address, which `piano-boot-update` writes from this file |
| `bluetooth-address` | the Bluetooth controller stays unconfigured |
| `boot-profile.json`, `boot-signing.pem` | `piano-boot-update` cannot rebuild the boot set, so the addresses above are not applied and kernel updates leave the set unchanged |
| `boot-partitions.json` | `piano-boot-switch` (switching from Ubuntu, the app-grid button) refuses; with piano-device-support 0.1+20261010.6 or later, kernel and initrd updates then leave the boot set and slot A unchanged |

After adding them run `sudo piano-boot-update $kernel_release`; it rebuilds the set with your addresses and
key and rewrites slot A. Your stock Android set goes into the store with
`sudo piano-boot-switch import-android --from <directory>`.

The prebuilt boot set fits stock `$stock_build` only. With another stock build, rebuild the set from
your own boot profile before writing slot A.

## Known issues

- No charging under Linux: keep at least 40 % and charge in Android.
- Release boots have no debug console. Developer mode (SSH over USB) is installed but off;
  enable it with `sudo piano-devmode on` before relying on remote access.
- With no picture or no response, hold Power + Volume Down for Fastboot and switch with `piano-switch.py`.
- `boot/boot-ram-maintenance.img` is for writing the partition table (INSTALL.md step 6) only: started with
  `fastboot boot`, it gives an unauthenticated root shell over the USB cable (telnet 192.168.88.2:2323) with the
  partition tools, mounts no UFS partition, writes nothing by itself and reboots into Fastboot after 10 minutes
  without a session. It is never written to a partition.
- The Android-side KernelSU module (`piano-switch-ksu`) was verified on the test unit with KernelSU-Next (install, `--check`, action to Ubuntu); other root managers are untested.
- Not flashed to a tablet as a whole: this image was assembled offline from the packages and
  procedure that run on the test unit, and checked offline. On the test unit, 7.2.6-piano4 booted as a RAM boot
  with the partition-name root and the serial-free command line of an address-free set, and the
  7.2.6-piano6 build of this release was installed from scratch there; `$kernel_release` has not been booted
  on a tablet yet.
  $qemu_note

## Source code

- Ubuntu: every Ubuntu binary in the root image is unmodified, from the official 26.04 Desktop ARM64 ISO
  and the archive snapshot https://snapshot.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/$apt_snapshot/ (packages listed above); the
  corresponding source is in the Ubuntu archive for the same versions.
- BusyBox: `piano-device-support` carries the unmodified Ubuntu `busybox-static` 1.36.1-6ubuntu3.1 binary;
  its source package (`busybox_1.36.1-6ubuntu3.1.dsc`, `busybox_1.36.1.orig.tar.bz2` with `.asc`,
  `busybox_1.36.1-6ubuntu3.1.debian.tar.xz`) is attached to this release.
- Kernel: https://github.com/yzddmr6/linux-sm8750-piano tag `v0.1.0-preview` (commit `$kernel_commit`).
- Mesa 26.2.0 with the A830 patch, device userspace and packaging: this repository at `$integration_commit`.

The firmware package carries unmodified stock files of `$stock_build`; there is no redistribution
licence for them, and they are removed on request of the rights holder. Integration code is MIT,
the kernel GPL-2.0.
